1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,108
You download a local AI model that

2
00:00:02,108 --> 00:00:04,725
promises a context window of one hundred

3
00:00:04,725 --> 00:00:07,760
and twenty-eight thousand tokens. That sounds enormous.

4
00:00:08,080 --> 00:00:10,443
Enough for a long report, a mountain

5
00:00:10,443 --> 00:00:13,138
of code, or an entire conversation that

6
00:00:13,138 --> 00:00:15,104
never seems to end. Then you load

7
00:00:15,104 --> 00:00:17,300
the model. You paste in a document.

8
00:00:17,620 --> 00:00:20,199
Your GPU memory fills up, the first

9
00:00:20,199 --> 00:00:23,272
answer takes forever, or the application refuses

10
00:00:23,272 --> 00:00:25,707
the request. And if it does answer,

11
00:00:25,707 --> 00:00:28,064
it confidently misses a detail you know

12
00:00:28,064 --> 00:00:29,610
is sitting right there in the text.

13
00:00:29,930 --> 00:00:31,910
What happened to those one hundred and twenty-eight

14
00:00:31,910 --> 00:00:35,217
thousand tokens? Here is the trap: a model

15
00:00:35,217 --> 00:00:38,173
can advertise a large context window without your

16
00:00:38,173 --> 00:00:41,030
computer being able to run that window efficiently.

17
00:00:41,350 --> 00:00:43,461
And even when the text fits, that

18
00:00:43,461 --> 00:00:45,062
does not guarantee the model will

19
00:00:45,062 --> 00:00:46,990
use every part of it correctly.

20
00:00:47,310 --> 00:00:49,546
Today we are following one long prompt

21
00:00:49,546 --> 00:00:51,864
through a local AI system, from the

22
00:00:51,864 --> 00:00:54,755
model specification to the GPU memory,

23
00:00:54,755 --> 00:00:56,070
all the way to the answer.

24
00:00:56,390 --> 00:00:58,089
We will find out what actually runs

25
00:00:58,089 --> 00:01:01,227
out, why long conversations slow down, and

26
00:01:01,227 --> 00:01:03,779
when a smaller, smarter context can beat

27
00:01:03,779 --> 00:01:06,344
a gigantic one. First, forget the

28
00:01:06,344 --> 00:01:08,890
idea that context is permanent memory.

29
00:01:09,210 --> 00:01:12,012
A context window is the working information

30
00:01:12,012 --> 00:01:13,848
made available to the model for a

31
00:01:13,848 --> 00:01:17,507
particular generation. It can contain instructions, prior

32
00:01:17,507 --> 00:01:20,697
messages, documents, tool results, and the

33
00:01:20,697 --> 00:01:22,810
beginning of the model's own response.

34
00:01:23,130 --> 00:01:25,839
A token is not necessarily one word.

35
00:01:25,839 --> 00:01:28,066
Depending on the tokenizer, a word can

36
00:01:28,066 --> 00:01:30,813
be one token, several tokens, or part

37
00:01:30,813 --> 00:01:34,524
of a token sequence. Punctuation, code, special

38
00:01:34,524 --> 00:01:37,200
chat markers, and formatting also count.

39
00:01:37,521 --> 00:01:40,633
So a one-hundred-page document cannot be converted into

40
00:01:40,633 --> 00:01:44,214
a reliable token count using page numbers alone.

41
00:01:44,214 --> 00:01:46,154
And the full context budget is not

42
00:01:46,154 --> 00:01:48,110
always yours to spend on the input.

43
00:01:48,430 --> 00:01:52,432
In many runtimes, prompt tokens and generated tokens

44
00:01:52,432 --> 00:01:55,426
share the configured context limit. If you fill

45
00:01:55,426 --> 00:01:58,122
the entire allowance with source material, you

46
00:01:58,122 --> 00:02:00,090
may leave no room for the answer.

47
00:02:00,412 --> 00:02:03,563
Separate model or application output limits may apply

48
00:02:03,563 --> 00:02:08,430
as well. So imagine a hypothetical one-hundred-and-twenty-eight-thousand-token budget.

49
00:02:08,750 --> 00:02:10,534
If you want the model to generate eight

50
00:02:10,534 --> 00:02:12,643
thousand tokens, you need to leave enough

51
00:02:12,643 --> 00:02:14,987
space for those eight thousand plus any

52
00:02:14,987 --> 00:02:18,310
system prompts, chat formatting, and other overhead.

53
00:02:18,630 --> 00:02:20,704
One hundred and twenty-eight thousand tokens is

54
00:02:20,704 --> 00:02:22,631
a ceiling, not a promise that you

55
00:02:22,631 --> 00:02:24,884
can paste in that much text and

56
00:02:24,884 --> 00:02:27,514
still receive a full response. Now comes

57
00:02:27,514 --> 00:02:29,380
the first of four separate limits.

58
00:02:29,700 --> 00:02:33,228
The model itself has a supported context configuration.

59
00:02:33,228 --> 00:02:35,832
That number may come from its architecture, training

60
00:02:35,832 --> 00:02:39,752
setup, and published configuration. Some models are designed

61
00:02:39,752 --> 00:02:42,370
or adapted for longer sequences than others.

62
00:02:42,690 --> 00:02:45,069
The second limit belongs to the software running

63
00:02:45,069 --> 00:02:47,985
the model. A local application may choose a

64
00:02:47,985 --> 00:02:51,370
smaller active context than the model's published maximum.

65
00:02:51,690 --> 00:02:54,023
Changing the model file does not force the

66
00:02:54,023 --> 00:02:57,187
application to use the largest supported value.

67
00:02:57,187 --> 00:03:01,978
For example, llama.cpp exposes a context-size setting.

68
00:03:01,978 --> 00:03:04,970
vLLM exposes a maximum model length setting.

69
00:03:05,290 --> 00:03:08,903
Both also have other configuration and memory constraints.

70
00:03:08,903 --> 00:03:11,416
The exact command names and behavior depend on

71
00:03:11,416 --> 00:03:13,380
which version and backend you are running.

72
00:03:13,700 --> 00:03:15,738
The important point is that the advertised

73
00:03:15,738 --> 00:03:18,277
model limit and the configured runtime limit

74
00:03:18,277 --> 00:03:20,894
are different facts. The third limit is

75
00:03:20,894 --> 00:03:23,850
physical memory. Not just the model weights.

76
00:03:23,850 --> 00:03:25,350
Not just the file you downloaded.

77
00:03:25,670 --> 00:03:27,840
The memory needed while the model reads

78
00:03:27,840 --> 00:03:30,587
and generates. This is where the key-value

79
00:03:30,587 --> 00:03:33,413
cache enters the story. When a transformer

80
00:03:33,413 --> 00:03:36,512
processes earlier tokens, its attention layers

81
00:03:36,512 --> 00:03:40,170
calculate representations called keys and values.

82
00:03:40,490 --> 00:03:45,102
During autoregressive generation, a supported caching implementation saves

83
00:03:45,102 --> 00:03:48,058
those representations so the model does not need

84
00:03:48,058 --> 00:03:50,988
to recompute the entire past in exactly

85
00:03:50,988 --> 00:03:53,300
the same way for every new token.

86
00:03:53,620 --> 00:03:55,220
Think of it as a growing set

87
00:03:55,220 --> 00:03:57,655
of working notes for the current request.

88
00:03:57,655 --> 00:03:59,210
The model weights are the machinery.

89
00:03:59,530 --> 00:04:02,024
The key-value cache is some of the temporary

90
00:04:02,024 --> 00:04:04,472
state the machinery needs to keep track

91
00:04:04,472 --> 00:04:07,401
of the conversation. For an ordinary full-attention

92
00:04:07,401 --> 00:04:10,917
transformer with a conventional cache, longer sequences

93
00:04:10,917 --> 00:04:13,500
need more stored key and value data.

94
00:04:13,820 --> 00:04:16,535
Cache memory scales with token count, with the

95
00:04:16,535 --> 00:04:19,282
number of layers, with the key-value heads and

96
00:04:19,282 --> 00:04:22,017
their dimensions, with the storage precision, and

97
00:04:22,017 --> 00:04:24,300
with how many sequences are being served.

98
00:04:24,627 --> 00:04:27,869
Here is a calculation, but listen carefully: this

99
00:04:27,869 --> 00:04:30,031
is a made-up architecture for explaining the

100
00:04:30,031 --> 00:04:32,309
math. It is not a measured benchmark

101
00:04:32,309 --> 00:04:35,170
for any GPU or named AI model.

102
00:04:35,490 --> 00:04:39,351
Imagine thirty-two layers, eight key-value heads per layer,

103
00:04:39,351 --> 00:04:41,760
one hundred and twenty-eight values per head, and

104
00:04:41,760 --> 00:04:44,676
two bytes for each stored value. Every cached

105
00:04:44,676 --> 00:04:47,010
token needs both a key and a value.

106
00:04:47,330 --> 00:04:51,556
With that hypothetical design, a thirty-two-thousand-token cache occupies

107
00:04:51,556 --> 00:04:55,801
about four gibibytes before implementation overhead and any

108
00:04:55,801 --> 00:04:59,694
other allocations. At eight thousand tokens, the same

109
00:04:59,694 --> 00:05:02,890
illustrative cache would occupy about one gibibyte.

110
00:05:03,210 --> 00:05:05,543
At one hundred and twenty-eight thousand tokens,

111
00:05:05,543 --> 00:05:07,630
it would be about sixteen gibibytes.

112
00:05:07,953 --> 00:05:09,880
That is the shock. The model weights

113
00:05:09,880 --> 00:05:12,068
could already fit comfortably on a graphics

114
00:05:12,068 --> 00:05:14,620
card, while the context cache pushes

115
00:05:14,620 --> 00:05:16,660
the whole workload over the edge.

116
00:05:16,980 --> 00:05:19,588
Reducing the weight file through quantization does

117
00:05:19,588 --> 00:05:22,257
not automatically reduce the cache to the

118
00:05:22,257 --> 00:05:25,460
same precision. The specific cache numbers change

119
00:05:25,460 --> 00:05:28,807
with the actual model architecture. Grouped-query attention

120
00:05:28,807 --> 00:05:31,420
can reduce the number of key-value heads.

121
00:05:31,740 --> 00:05:34,806
Sliding-window and other attention patterns can change

122
00:05:34,806 --> 00:05:37,449
which tokens a layer needs to retain.

123
00:05:37,449 --> 00:05:41,212
Some runtimes use quantized or offloaded caches.

124
00:05:41,212 --> 00:05:44,038
So never copy those four-gibibyte numbers onto

125
00:05:44,038 --> 00:05:47,120
an unrelated model and call them specifications.

126
00:05:47,440 --> 00:05:50,021
But the principle is real: fitting the weights

127
00:05:50,021 --> 00:05:52,105
is not the same as fitting a long

128
00:05:52,105 --> 00:05:54,761
conversation. Now think about what happens when the

129
00:05:54,761 --> 00:05:57,770
runtime is also serving more than one request.

130
00:05:58,090 --> 00:06:00,189
A chat server might hold active state

131
00:06:00,189 --> 00:06:03,249
for multiple users. Depending on its scheduling

132
00:06:03,249 --> 00:06:05,879
and cache design, the available memory

133
00:06:05,879 --> 00:06:08,460
must support those active sequences too.

134
00:06:08,780 --> 00:06:10,682
A context size that works for one

135
00:06:10,682 --> 00:06:12,870
short chat may not be practical with

136
00:06:12,870 --> 00:06:15,070
long requests running at the same time.

137
00:06:15,390 --> 00:06:17,311
This is why you sometimes see errors

138
00:06:17,311 --> 00:06:20,020
about insufficient cache memory even after the

139
00:06:20,020 --> 00:06:22,259
weights have loaded. And it's why blindly

140
00:06:22,259 --> 00:06:23,978
raising the context setting can make

141
00:06:23,978 --> 00:06:26,430
a previously stable system stop launching.

142
00:06:26,750 --> 00:06:29,215
The fourth limit is more subtle. Even if

143
00:06:29,215 --> 00:06:32,301
the model supports the tokens, the runtime accepts

144
00:06:32,301 --> 00:06:34,905
them, and the hardware stores them, the model

145
00:06:34,905 --> 00:06:37,720
may not use all that information reliably.

146
00:06:38,040 --> 00:06:40,476
Long-context research has demonstrated that the

147
00:06:40,476 --> 00:06:42,598
position of a fact can influence

148
00:06:42,598 --> 00:06:44,770
whether a model retrieves it correctly.

149
00:06:45,090 --> 00:06:47,245
In the study called Lost in the Middle,

150
00:06:47,245 --> 00:06:50,396
the researchers found cases where information near the

151
00:06:50,396 --> 00:06:52,401
beginning or the end of a long

152
00:06:52,401 --> 00:06:54,966
input was easier for tested models to

153
00:06:54,966 --> 00:06:57,110
use than information buried in the middle.

154
00:06:57,430 --> 00:06:58,879
That is not a claim that every

155
00:06:58,879 --> 00:07:01,925
modern model always forgets the middle. Results

156
00:07:01,925 --> 00:07:04,880
differ by model, task, and evaluation.

157
00:07:05,200 --> 00:07:06,567
It is a warning that a large

158
00:07:06,567 --> 00:07:09,393
numerical context limit is not the same

159
00:07:09,393 --> 00:07:12,557
as reliable recall across the entire window.

160
00:07:12,557 --> 00:07:14,920
Imagine giving a model forty reports.

161
00:07:15,240 --> 00:07:18,371
One report, buried halfway through, contains the

162
00:07:18,371 --> 00:07:21,041
single number that answers your question. You

163
00:07:21,041 --> 00:07:22,968
may discover that changing the order of

164
00:07:22,968 --> 00:07:25,559
those reports changes the answer, even

165
00:07:25,559 --> 00:07:27,260
though none of the facts changed.

166
00:07:27,580 --> 00:07:28,844
This is why one of the most

167
00:07:28,844 --> 00:07:31,904
useful long-context tests is not asking whether

168
00:07:31,904 --> 00:07:33,880
the model loads a giant document.

169
00:07:34,200 --> 00:07:36,649
It is placing the same important detail near

170
00:07:36,649 --> 00:07:39,526
the beginning, the middle, and the end, and

171
00:07:39,526 --> 00:07:42,261
checking whether the answers stay correct. Now let's

172
00:07:42,261 --> 00:07:44,780
follow the clock, not just the memory meter.

173
00:07:45,100 --> 00:07:46,927
Before a model can produce the first

174
00:07:46,927 --> 00:07:49,010
answer token, it must process the

175
00:07:49,010 --> 00:07:51,549
input prompt. That initial pass is

176
00:07:51,549 --> 00:07:54,170
often called prefill or prompt processing.

177
00:07:54,490 --> 00:07:57,254
A very long prompt can demand substantial

178
00:07:57,254 --> 00:07:59,924
computation and data movement, even when the

179
00:07:59,924 --> 00:08:01,747
final answer will only be a few

180
00:08:01,747 --> 00:08:05,366
sentences. Then comes decoding: generating output

181
00:08:05,366 --> 00:08:08,140
tokens, usually one step after another.

182
00:08:08,460 --> 00:08:11,097
The cache helps by reusing prior key and

183
00:08:11,097 --> 00:08:13,219
value states, but it does not make

184
00:08:13,219 --> 00:08:16,123
a very long context free. Attention and

185
00:08:16,123 --> 00:08:19,310
memory traffic still matter, and implementations vary.

186
00:08:19,631 --> 00:08:21,323
That gives you two separate speeds to

187
00:08:21,323 --> 00:08:23,198
measure. How long did it take to

188
00:08:23,198 --> 00:08:25,269
get the first token? And how quickly

189
00:08:25,269 --> 00:08:27,900
did the answer arrive after generation started?

190
00:08:28,220 --> 00:08:30,781
A system can generate tokens quickly once it

191
00:08:30,781 --> 00:08:33,581
begins, yet feel painfully slow because it

192
00:08:33,581 --> 00:08:36,068
spent so long reading the prompt. There

193
00:08:36,068 --> 00:08:39,570
are clever optimizations. Flash Attention and related

194
00:08:39,570 --> 00:08:43,050
kernels reduce certain memory and computation costs.

195
00:08:43,370 --> 00:08:45,964
Cache quantization can save space on supported

196
00:08:45,964 --> 00:08:49,207
backends. Prefix caching can reuse work when

197
00:08:49,207 --> 00:08:52,449
requests share an identical prefix. Some engines

198
00:08:52,449 --> 00:08:54,460
can move cache data to other memory.

199
00:08:54,780 --> 00:08:56,740
But none of these features automatically

200
00:08:56,740 --> 00:08:59,631
turns the largest advertised context into

201
00:08:59,631 --> 00:09:01,840
the best setting for every task.

202
00:09:02,160 --> 00:09:03,908
And there is another trap: making the

203
00:09:03,908 --> 00:09:07,189
context longer through positional-scaling settings is not

204
00:09:07,189 --> 00:09:09,156
the same as proving that the model

205
00:09:09,156 --> 00:09:11,708
remains accurate at the new length. A

206
00:09:11,708 --> 00:09:14,700
runtime may let you change positional parameters.

207
00:09:15,020 --> 00:09:16,710
That does not guarantee the model was

208
00:09:16,710 --> 00:09:19,861
trained or validated to reason reliably across

209
00:09:19,861 --> 00:09:22,100
that expanded window. So what should you

210
00:09:22,100 --> 00:09:24,861
do instead of immediately setting everything

211
00:09:24,861 --> 00:09:26,450
to one hundred and twenty-eight thousand?

212
00:09:26,812 --> 00:09:29,690
First, find the real context limit published

213
00:09:29,690 --> 00:09:31,799
for the exact model version, and check

214
00:09:31,799 --> 00:09:35,067
the running application's configured limit. Record both

215
00:09:35,067 --> 00:09:37,112
numbers. Do not assume the model

216
00:09:37,112 --> 00:09:39,090
page and the software settings agree.

217
00:09:39,410 --> 00:09:43,034
Second, inspect the runtime logs. Check where model

218
00:09:43,034 --> 00:09:45,664
weights are loaded, how much cache memory is

219
00:09:45,664 --> 00:09:49,114
allocated, which cache precision is active, and whether

220
00:09:49,114 --> 00:09:51,290
any memory has been placed on the CPU.

221
00:09:51,610 --> 00:09:54,123
If your interface hides those details, use

222
00:09:54,123 --> 00:09:58,172
the backend logs or supported diagnostics. Third,

223
00:09:58,172 --> 00:09:59,800
test the workload you actually care

224
00:09:59,800 --> 00:10:02,140
about. Start with a modest context.

225
00:10:02,460 --> 00:10:04,233
Then increase it in steps using the

226
00:10:04,233 --> 00:10:06,889
same model, the same quantization, and the

227
00:10:06,889 --> 00:10:11,511
same questions. Record available VRAM, the prompt-processing

228
00:10:11,511 --> 00:10:14,376
time, time to first token, and output

229
00:10:14,376 --> 00:10:17,530
generation speed. Fourth, test answer quality.

230
00:10:17,855 --> 00:10:20,199
Put one known fact near the start,

231
00:10:20,199 --> 00:10:21,631
one in the middle, and one at

232
00:10:21,631 --> 00:10:23,923
the end of a document. Ask precise

233
00:10:23,923 --> 00:10:27,217
questions with answers you can independently verify.

234
00:10:27,217 --> 00:10:28,890
Repeat with more than one example.

235
00:10:29,210 --> 00:10:31,830
A successful model load is not a successful

236
00:10:31,830 --> 00:10:34,877
comprehension test. Fifth, reserve space for the

237
00:10:34,877 --> 00:10:38,926
answer. Count the system instructions, previous messages,

238
00:10:38,926 --> 00:10:42,790
inserted documents, tool results, and expected output.

239
00:10:43,110 --> 00:10:46,639
If the application silently truncates older messages or

240
00:10:46,639 --> 00:10:50,077
shifts the context, learn exactly what it keeps

241
00:10:50,077 --> 00:10:53,280
and what it discards. And sixth, question whether

242
00:10:53,280 --> 00:10:55,820
the model needs the whole document at once.

243
00:10:56,153 --> 00:10:58,509
For searching a knowledge base, a retrieval

244
00:10:58,509 --> 00:11:00,957
system can locate a smaller collection of

245
00:11:00,957 --> 00:11:03,939
relevant passages and pass those passages to

246
00:11:03,939 --> 00:11:05,723
the model. That is the basic

247
00:11:05,723 --> 00:11:09,510
idea behind retrieval-augmented generation, or RAG.

248
00:11:09,830 --> 00:11:12,216
It is not a magic cure. Retrieval can

249
00:11:12,216 --> 00:11:16,018
select the wrong passages, omit a crucial detail,

250
00:11:16,018 --> 00:11:18,880
or lose context needed to interpret a quotation.

251
00:11:19,200 --> 00:11:20,675
But when the task is answering a

252
00:11:20,675 --> 00:11:23,410
question from a large collection of documents,

253
00:11:23,410 --> 00:11:26,014
focused retrieval may be more useful than

254
00:11:26,014 --> 00:11:28,340
pouring every page into the prompt.

255
00:11:28,715 --> 00:11:32,882
For whole-book editing, complex cross-document reasoning, or questions

256
00:11:32,882 --> 00:11:35,434
that depend on broad structure, you may still

257
00:11:35,434 --> 00:11:39,420
need larger context, careful chunking, or multiple passes.

258
00:11:39,740 --> 00:11:42,495
The correct strategy depends on the task, not

259
00:11:42,495 --> 00:11:44,995
on whichever context number looks biggest in a

260
00:11:44,995 --> 00:11:48,570
model comparison table. And one final distinction matters.

261
00:11:48,890 --> 00:11:51,240
Saving a chat transcript on disk is

262
00:11:51,240 --> 00:11:53,558
not the same as keeping every line

263
00:11:53,558 --> 00:11:56,917
inside the model's active context. Your application

264
00:11:56,917 --> 00:11:59,742
might retrieve, summarize, or drop older

265
00:11:59,742 --> 00:12:02,110
material. That can be useful engineering.

266
00:12:02,430 --> 00:12:04,598
But if it isn't transparent, the model

267
00:12:04,598 --> 00:12:07,215
can seem to remember something one moment

268
00:12:07,215 --> 00:12:09,025
and forget it the next. So here

269
00:12:09,025 --> 00:12:10,835
is the verdict. There is a model

270
00:12:10,835 --> 00:12:12,900
limit. There is a runtime limit.

271
00:12:13,220 --> 00:12:15,012
There is a hardware limit. And there

272
00:12:15,012 --> 00:12:17,889
is an effective-use limit. Those four numbers

273
00:12:17,889 --> 00:12:21,405
do not have to match. A one-hundred-and-twenty-eight-thousand-token

274
00:12:21,405 --> 00:12:24,020
label can describe a supported capability.

275
00:12:24,340 --> 00:12:26,550
It does not promise that your current GPU

276
00:12:26,550 --> 00:12:29,571
has enough memory, that your interface is configured

277
00:12:29,571 --> 00:12:31,550
to use it, that the first answer

278
00:12:31,550 --> 00:12:34,193
will arrive quickly, or that every fact

279
00:12:34,193 --> 00:12:36,570
inside the window will be retrieved correctly.

280
00:12:36,890 --> 00:12:39,975
Before buying more hardware or changing every advanced

281
00:12:39,975 --> 00:12:43,061
setting, measure the real bottleneck. Is the prompt

282
00:12:43,061 --> 00:12:45,340
being truncated? Is the cache running out

283
00:12:45,340 --> 00:12:47,750
of memory? Is prefill taking too long?

284
00:12:48,070 --> 00:12:50,477
Or is the answer simply wrong even though

285
00:12:50,477 --> 00:12:53,355
all the evidence fits? Those problems require

286
00:12:53,355 --> 00:12:56,037
different solutions. And solving the wrong one

287
00:12:56,037 --> 00:12:58,381
can cost you hours, money, or the

288
00:12:58,381 --> 00:13:00,650
very accuracy you were trying to improve.

289
00:13:00,970 --> 00:13:03,209
Tell me the exact local model, your

290
00:13:03,209 --> 00:13:06,126
GPU, the context setting you use, and

291
00:13:06,126 --> 00:13:07,441
what happens when you feed it a

292
00:13:07,441 --> 00:13:10,527
long prompt. Does it crash, crawl,

293
00:13:10,527 --> 00:13:12,420
truncate, or give the wrong answer?

294
00:13:12,740 --> 00:13:14,831
This is Byte Forensics. Don't just

295
00:13:14,831 --> 00:13:16,771
count the tokens. Find out which

296
00:13:16,771 --> 00:13:19,590
ones your system can actually use.
